            <ArticleTitle>Connexin37 gene polymorphism and coronary artery disease in Taiwan.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>251-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A recent study shows that a C1019T polymorphism of the gene encoding the gap junction protein connexin37 contributes to the genesis of atherosclerotic plaques in human carotid artery. However, whether such a polymorphism can be used as a prognostic marker in atherosclerotic disease of other arterial sites, such as coronary artery disease, is not known.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We analyzed the allelic status in 177 subjects with coronary artery disease (age, 61+/-11 years; male/female, 120/57) and 102 controls (60+/-11 years; male/female, 70/32). Both groups were matched, before genotype analysis, for a variety of other traditional risk factors, including body mass index, smoking status, levels of blood pressure, sugar, creatinine, and lipid profiles, in addition to age and sex.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The T allele was less frequently seen in the control group, compared to the disease group (10.7 vs. 20.1%, TT+TC vs. CC, P&lt;0.01). Subsequent analysis demonstrated that a significant difference existed in the male (9.2 vs. 22.8%, TT+TC vs. CC, P&lt;0.005), but not in the female. Another finding was that the T allele frequency in all participants was less than 15%, markedly lower than that reported in non-Taiwanese.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The observation indicates that the polymorphism in the connexin37 gene potentially plays a role in the manifestation of coronary atherosclerosis in Taiwan.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
